[
  {
    "knowledge_point": {
      "db_id": 17592186092384,
      "id": "4a05f6c0-98ab-5197-922e-8e8dff1d4302",
      "title": "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{(x-3)^2}{\\sqrt{x}},\\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(4,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=-\\frac{(x-3)^2}{\\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=\\frac{-x^{2}+6x-9}{x^{1/2}}\\\\\n&=-x^{\\frac{3}{2}}+6x^{\\frac{1}{2}}-9x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=-\\frac{3}{2}x^{\\frac{1}{2}}+3x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+\\frac{9}{2}x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(4,-\\frac{1}{2}\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(4)&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(4\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}+3\\left(4\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+\\frac{9}{2}\\left(4\\right)^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\\n&=-3+\\frac{3}{2}+\\frac{9}{16}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{15}{16}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{15}{16}\\right)}\\\\\n&=\\frac{16}{15}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
            ":answer/id",
            "bc894574-064a-5a40-a551-026f5aac56a4"
          ],
          ":answer-field/choices": [
            {
              ":answer/id": "bc894574-064a-5a40-a551-026f5aac56a4",
              ":answer/type": ":answer.type/math",
              ":answer/value": "\\frac{16}{15}"
            }
          ],
          ":answer-field/id": "2267e857-aa87-5e82-ac21-3bfa172671c7"
        }
      ],
