      "title": "Calculating the Slope of a Normal Line to a Curve at a Point"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=-\\frac{(x+1)^2}{\\sqrt{x}},\\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(9,-\\frac{100}{3}\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=-\\frac{(x+1)^2}{\\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=\\frac{-x^{2}-2x-1}{x^{1/2}}\\\\\n&=-x^{\\frac{3}{2}}-2x^{\\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=-\\frac{3}{2}x^{\\frac{1}{2}}-x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(9,-\\frac{100}{3}\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(9)&=-\\frac{3}{2}\\left(9\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}-\\left(9\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}\\left(9\\right)^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{9}{2}-\\frac{1}{3}+\\frac{1}{54}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{130}{27}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(-\\frac{130}{27}\\right)}\\\\\n&=\\frac{27}{130}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
          ":answer-field/correct": [
