    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Find the slope $m'$ of the normal line to the curve\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{(x+2)^2}{\\sqrt{x}},\\qquad x>0\n$$\n\nat the point $\\left(1,9\\right)$. Give an exact answer.\n\n$m'=$\n{{field-1}}",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Let $f(x)=\\frac{(x+2)^2}{\\sqrt{x}}$. Expand the numerator and divide each term by $x^{1/2}$.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nf(x)&=\\frac{x^{2}+4x+4}{x^{1/2}}\\\\\n&=x^{\\frac{3}{2}}+4x^{\\frac{1}{2}}+4x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nApply the power rule:\n\n$$\nf'(x)=\\frac{3}{2}x^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2x^{-\\frac{1}{2}}-2x^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\n$$\n\nThe tangent slope at $\\left(1,9\\right)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm=f'(1)&=\\frac{3}{2}\\left(1\\right)^{\\frac{1}{2}}+2\\left(1\\right)^{-\\frac{1}{2}}-2\\left(1\\right)^{-\\frac{3}{2}}\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}+2-2\\\\\n&=\\frac{3}{2}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe normal is perpendicular to the tangent, so take the negative reciprocal:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\nm'&=-\\frac{1}{m}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{1}{\\left(\\frac{3}{2}\\right)}\\\\\n&=-\\frac{2}{3}\n\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/answer-fields": [
        {
          ":answer-field/key": "field-1",
          ":answer-field/type": ":answer-field.type/blank",
